# Operator configuration for the spelltype single-runtime stack (compose.yaml,
# optional deploy/compose.dokploy.yaml) and the deploy CLI (scripts/deploy.ts).
# Copy to deploy/compose.env and fill in. Never commit the real file; secrets
# live as 0600 files under SPELLTYPE_STATE_DIR/secrets.
#
# AUTHORIZATION MODEL (WeChat roles; optional maintenance-only CI credential):
#   - The deploy CLI uses the host's existing Docker privilege: maintenance
#     operations run one-shot INSIDE the app container
#     (`docker compose exec -T app bun dist/server/maintenance.js ...`), or as
#     a one-shot container from the same image while no app exists. The app
#     never mounts the Docker socket and never receives an admin secret.
#   - Local lifecycle commands prove the runtime through public /health (live
#     lease + database) and /api/status; its runtimeEpoch is required to resume.
#     There is no separate admin listener or port.
#   - In-app administration (GET/POST /api/admin/maintenance) is restricted to
#     the WeChat admin ROLE on the normal app; it is unrelated to deploys.
#   - OPTIONAL machine API for CI/platform automation: a scoped bearer token
#     (MAINTENANCE_TOKEN) enables /api/ops/maintenance (status/drain/wait/
#     resume only — it never replaces containers and authorizes nothing
#     else). See "REMOTE MAINTENANCE API" below; absent token = ops API off.
#
# CREDENTIALS / FIRST SETUP (from the repo root):
#   1. cp deploy/compose.env.example deploy/compose.env and edit the values below.
#   2. Export the secret inputs (marked "secret input" below), then:
#        bun run deploy secrets
#      Secret files are created with mode 0600. Existing files are left
#      unchanged (never overwritten), so re-running after exporting an
#      additional secret (e.g. MAINTENANCE_TOKEN) only adds what is missing.
#      To rotate a secret, delete its file deliberately and re-run.
#   3. Initial install — works when no app container exists yet:
#        bun run deploy build                      # build the candidate image; prints its image ID
#        bun run deploy install --image <imageId>  # database -> migrate (durable draining) -> app -> /health -> resume
#   4. Ordinary updates (one command; the candidate is built and verified
#      before any maintenance, so a broken build can never close the service):
#        bun run deploy build
#        bun run deploy deploy --image <imageId> [--wait-timeout <s>]
#      Sequence: verify candidate entries executable + build identity -> enter
#      draining (durable revision CAS) -> poll until every match has finished
#      (bounded timeout; on timeout the deploy aborts, games keep running
#      untouched and maintenance stays closed) -> stop the old app container
#      (only after fully drained, revision unchanged — never while a newer
#      global runtime owner exists) -> migrate once with the new image ->
#      start the new app -> verify /health + /api/status (protocol and build
#      identity) -> resume admission via revision + runtime-epoch CAS.
#      Zero-overlap: exactly one app container exists at any time; the old one
#      always stops before the new one starts, and --no-deps/--force-recreate
#      guarantee no simultaneous old/new pair.
#      Candidate startup/health failures do not reopen admission. If activation
#      acknowledgment is lost, inspect durable status before recovery.
#   5. Rollback — explicit, never automatic, never down-migrates the schema:
#        bun run deploy rollback --schema-compatible
#      Restores the exact previous healthy image ID. If replacement failed,
#      the pending-operation journal instead selects the last healthy current
#      image (pass --image <imageId> for an explicit target). It re-drains
#      a running healthy app first, never runs migrations, and requires
#      --schema-compatible: the operator asserts the previous build tolerates
#      the CURRENT forward schema (true for additive migrations). If the
#      database is still at the previous level (a deploy that failed before
#      migrate), rollback is trivially safe.
#
# IMAGE IMMUTABILITY:
#   The CLI pins an exact image ID (sha256:...) into compose on every
#   operation, verifies the actual running container image, and records it in
#   <state>/deploy-state.json. A pending target is written BEFORE the old app
#   stops; healthy image history is committed BEFORE admission reopens.
#   For registry production, pass a digest pin (`--image registry.example.com/spelltype@sha256:...`);
#   local verified image IDs are acceptable for explicit local smoke. Never
#   reference mutable tags.
#
# MAINTENANCE OPERATIONS (separate from deploys; each runs one-shot inside
# the running app container unless --http is given):
#   bun run maintenance status
#   bun run maintenance drain [--timeout <s>]   # close admission, wait until drained
#   bun run maintenance wait  [--timeout <s>]   # bounded, mutation-free readiness wait;
#                                               # returns only on ready + unchanged draining revision
#   bun run maintenance resume                  # reopen admission (revision + epoch CAS)
#   bun run deploy status                       # compose state + maintenance + runtime proof + image history
#
# REMOTE MAINTENANCE API (CI / platform automation, no Docker needed):
#   A scoped bearer token enables GET/POST /api/ops/maintenance on the normal
#   app listener: GET returns the DrainStatus; POST {mode:'draining',
#   expectedRevision} closes admission; POST {mode:'open',expectedRevision,
#   expectedRuntimeEpoch} reopens it (the server verifies the epoch against
#   its live runtime lease). Setup:
#     1. Add deploy/compose.ops.yaml to COMPOSE_FILE (see that file).
#     2. MAINTENANCE_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 32) bun run deploy secrets
#     3. The token reaches the app at the next container recreate (initial
#        install or the next `bun run deploy deploy`).
#   Remote usage (token via environment or file, never argv, never logged):
#     SPELLTYPE_OPS_URL=https://spelltype.example.com \
#       MAINTENANCE_TOKEN_FILE=<path> bun run maintenance drain --http
#     ... bun run maintenance wait --http --timeout 900
#     ... <CI/platform replaces the container itself> ...
#     ... bun run maintenance resume --http
#   The remote API never replaces containers and never needs Docker; the
#   platform owns the Docker action and must start the new app only while
#   maintenance remains draining (resume only after its /health proves the
#   new runtime lease).
#   Remote origins must use HTTPS (HTTP is allowed only on loopback for local
#   verification); credentials, paths, query strings and redirects are refused.
#   Resume obtains the epoch from /health, not from the drain counter response.
#
# LEGACY CUTOVER (old release-based host; run once, in this order):
#   The first migration refuses to run while the old stack still has active
#   games, live reservations, pending results or a live legacy runtime lease.
#     1. Drain and stop the OLD stack with the old tooling before this point.
#        A graceful old-runtime shutdown clears its lease immediately; after a
#        crash the lease expires within 30 seconds without a heartbeat.
#     2. Take a database backup, e.g.:
#          docker compose -p <old-project> exec database \
#            pg_dump -U spelltype spelltype > spelltype-backup.sql
#     3. `bun run deploy secrets` then `bun run deploy install --image <id>`.
#   No script here erases volumes; deleting an OLD project's volumes remains
#   a separate, manual, operator-owned decision. There is no automatic schema
#   downgrade anywhere in this tooling.
#
# DOKPLOY HOSTS:
#   Set COMPOSE_FILE below to compose.yaml:deploy/compose.dokploy.yaml and set
#   SPELLTYPE_DOKPLOY_HOST. deploy/compose.dokploy.yaml is the authoritative
#   Traefik routing source, so CLI deploys/rollbacks (container recreates)
#   always preserve domain routing and TLS labels. Provision the project once,
#   then disable Dokploy UI auto-deploy/webhook triggers for this service:
#   scripts/deploy.ts is the single owner of the app lifecycle. The CLI runs
#   on the host (e.g. over SSH) and reaches the app only through the loopback
#   port below and docker compose exec.

# Absolute host path holding secrets/ and deploy-state.json.
# Created by `bun run deploy secrets`; must exist before first up.
SPELLTYPE_STATE_DIR=/srv/spelltype

# Canonical public origin served at the routing domain (no path, query or fragment).
SPELLTYPE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://spelltype.example.com

# xsg-website WeChat bridge (HTTPS in production). Create an App under
# xsg-website /developers: it issues the App ID and App Key used below, and its
# allowed return hosts (configured per App in that portal) must include
# SPELLTYPE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN's host; the bridge's development mode instead allows
# local hosts. Our callback is /api/auth/wechat/callback. The App ID is public
# and is NOT a WeChat AppID; the App Key is server-side only and never reaches
# the browser.
WECHAT_BRIDGE_BASE_URL=https://bridge.example.com
# Public App ID issued by the xsg-website /developers App (leave empty until the
# App exists; compose refuses to interpolate an unset App ID).
WECHAT_BRIDGE_APP_ID=
# secret input: App Key from the same xsg-website /developers App, 16+ characters.
# WECHAT_BRIDGE_APP_KEY

# Standard compose file set; the CLI passes it to every docker compose call so
# operator and CLI always see identical routing/networks. Plain Compose host
# (no Dokploy): delete this line and the SPELLTYPE_DOKPLOY_* lines below.
COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:deploy/compose.dokploy.yaml

# Public domain routed by the host Traefik to app:3000 (Dokploy override only).
SPELLTYPE_DOKPLOY_HOST=spelltype.example.com
# Traefik label names/entrypoints when the host differs from Dokploy defaults.
# SPELLTYPE_TRAEFIK_ROUTER=spelltype
# SPELLTYPE_TRAEFIK_ENTRYPOINTS=websecure
# SPELLTYPE_TRAEFIK_CERTRESOLVER=letsencrypt

# Compose project name; change it to run an isolated acceptance stack beside a
# live deployment (its postgres volume and network are project-scoped).
SPELLTYPE_PROJECT=spelltype

# Host port published on loopback only, for the CLI's public /health and
# /api/status probes. Dokploy domain routing reaches app:3000 over the
# internal network and never uses this; move it if the port is taken.
# SPELLTYPE_APP_HOST_PORT=3000

# Uid/Gid the app and one-shot containers run as. File secrets preserve host
# ownership, so these must match the user that runs `bun run deploy secrets`
# (creates the 0600 files): Linux deploy user 1000/1000, a macOS admin host
# typically 501/20.
SPELLTYPE_UID=1000
SPELLTYPE_GID=1000

# Database identity used for the database_url secret and the postgres container.
POSTGRES_USER=spelltype
POSTGRES_DB=spelltype
# secret input: database superuser password.
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD

# secret input: DeepSeek API key for spell generation.
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY
DEEPSEEK_MODEL=deepseek-flash

# optional secret input: machine token for the /api/ops/maintenance API,
# 64 lowercase hex (openssl rand -hex 32). Export only when the remote ops
# transport is wanted; without the file the ops API is disabled.
# MAINTENANCE_TOKEN

# Game policy for newly started containers; existing matches keep their
# recorded policy and deploys never recreate containers to change it.
INPUT_POLICY_MODE=observe
# Use draining via `bun run maintenance drain` to stop new matches while
# calibrating; enforce rejects completions below the timing floor. Leave and
# cancel remain available during maintenance. Reporting reads the database
# directly (not the API): bun scripts/input-policy-report.ts --from-ms <ms> --to-ms <ms>

# Client IP for authentication throttling: unset/empty/false uses the connection IP.
# true reads the rightmost X-Forwarded-For entry; any other value names a single-IP header.
# Missing/empty headers fall back to the connection IP. Header names are case-insensitive.
# Configure only behind an ingress that overwrites the selected header and cannot be bypassed.
# TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR=true
# EdgeOne example (instead of true):
# TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR=EO-Client-IP

# The deploy CLI pins SPELLTYPE_APP_IMAGE (exact image ID) via the process
# environment on every command; a bare `docker compose up` must export it
# explicitly. Never set it to a mutable tag in this file.
# SPELLTYPE_APP_IMAGE=sha256:...
